  "response": "Zoologists adjudicate between rival accounts of a mass mortality event by requiring each candidate explanation to predict something recoverable from the field, and then gathering that material under a protocol fixed before the explanations are weighed. The 2015 collapse of the Betpak-dala saiga population in central Kazakhstan, in which well over a hundred thousand animals died within weeks, shows how the procedure runs. That the pathological diagnosis in the affected animals was haemorrhagic septicaemia is confirmed by the field necropsies that veterinarians carried out on carcasses within days of death. Only carcasses handled on that timescale support the histology, culture and biochemistry on which every later discrimination between hypotheses rests.\n\nPasteurella multocida serotype B was isolated from the tissues of dead saiga, and Pasteurella multocida is carried as a commensal organism in the tonsils of healthy saiga; recovery of the serotype from the tissues of animals that died rules out its being a harmless tonsillar resident of healthy ones, so the two results cannot both be true. The recovery of that serotype from the fatal cases confirms the septicaemic diagnosis returned at necropsy. Bacillus anthracis was identified as the agent responsible for the 2015 saiga die-off, and that identification cannot be squared with the isolation of Pasteurella multocida serotype B from the dead animals: the two attributions cannot both be true, and the choice between them turns on culture and sequence data rather than on the narrative appeal of either. Lesion distribution and bacterial load, not the identity of the isolate alone, carry the inference.\n\nThe second line of adjudication reconstructs the conditions under which the population was living when it collapsed. Unusually high humidity and warm temperatures were recorded in the days preceding the mortality. Earlier mass die-offs of saiga in Kazakhstan occurred in 1981 and 1988. The humid warmth registered immediately before the mortality caused the haemorrhagic septicaemia found in the affected animals. Station records and the archive of documented events supply the baseline against which 2015 is positioned. Accounts invoking contamination are held to the same evidential standard as accounts invoking a pathogen. Rocket fuel contamination from the Baikonur Cosmodrome was advanced as an explanation for the deaths. Toxicological analyses were performed on forage plants and surface water from the die-off sites. Sampling the pasture itself, and not the carcasses alone, establishes whether a candidate compound was ever available in quantity for the animals to take up.\n\nThe toxicological arm of the inquiry narrows the field decisively. Lead concentrations measured in saiga liver tissue exceeded lethal thresholds, a result that rules out any wholly negative toxicological screen of those tissues. Either the screening of saiga tissues detected no toxin at a lethal concentration, or it detected at least one toxin at such a concentration: one of the two must hold, and not both. The negative outcome shows that the animals that died were infected with a bacterial pathogen, while the positive outcome indicates that they had ingested a toxic compound from their environment.\n\nWhat the combined record secures is that at least one of the two conditions obtained in the animals that died, infection with a bacterial pathogen or ingestion of a toxic compound from their environment, and the two are not mutually exclusive. Adjudication of this kind ends not in a single decisive test but in the convergence of pathology, microbiology, environmental monitoring and toxicology, each line constraining what the others can be taken to mean.",
